{"qid":"Q66504140","names":["Habsburg Monarchy"],"title":"Habsburg Monarchy","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Habsburg Monarchy was the dynastic complex of territories ruled by the House of Habsburg from their acquisition of the Austrian duchies (formally consolidated under Maximilian I after 1490) through the abolition of the Holy Roman Empire in 1806. At its broadest, under Charles V (r. 1519–1556), the dynasty simultaneously held the Spanish kingdoms, the Italian dependencies, the Low Countries, and the Austrian hereditary lands; the permanent division after 1556 into Spanish and Austrian branches left the latter ruling Austria, Bohemia, and Hungary from Vienna. The Napoleonic Wars stripped the Habsburgs of their imperial title in 1806, though the Austrian Empire was proclaimed in 1804 as a successor state under Francis II.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Austrian Habsburg lands are documented through imperial administrative records and the land registers of Bohemia, Austria, and Hungary. Boundaries of the composite monarchy shifted frequently through inheritance, conquest, and cession; the geometry represents the Austrian branch's territories and is moderately well-attested for the core lands but approximated for contested border zones.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: House of Habsburg","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/House-of-Habsburg"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Habsburg Austria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habsburg_Austria"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Habsburg Austria","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Rudolf I (as King of Germany/Archduke)","nativeNam